<h2>The question</h2>
<p>
  The review system has accumulated dozens of tuning knobs — care levels, lens rosters, juror counts, round
  caps, impact bars, evidence floors, land modes — added one ruling at a time, each argued on its own merits.
  Nobody has ever listed them together. <strong>What are all the levers, what is each one's range, who sets
  it today, and which of them is a platform invariant rather than a per-consumer setting?</strong>
</p>
<p>
  The framing is not new here. It is
  <a href="/backlog/">the repo's own <code>#config-extends-platform-default</code> rule</a> — a concern with
  more than one legitimate end-state is a configurable dimension, not a fork — applied to the whole review
  system at once rather than to one question at a time.
</p>

<h2>The proposed test — and why it was withdrawn</h2>
<p class="note">
  <strong>Read this section as a failed proposal, not a rule.</strong> The four-question test below was drafted
  as this survey's main deliverable and <strong>refuted by its own validation</strong>: run on a ruling made one
  day earlier in the same cluster, it returns the opposite answer. It is kept in full, with the refutation,
  because a discarded classifier is worth more on the record than a silent deletion — and because the reason it
  failed names a third exclusion ground the repo was already using and had never written down.
</p>
<p>
  The natural test is binary: <em>a lever is a setting when two consumers choosing differently both produce
  coherent systems; it is an invariant when one choice makes the system contradict itself.</em> That test
  fails on the first real case.
</p>
<p>
  The advisory-blocking decision found that per-<strong>surface</strong> variation in <em>seating</em> is
  legitimate and already ruled, while per-surface variation in <em>blocking power</em> would "let one gate
  stop a PR that another gate clears on the identical finding, which is incoherence rather than
  configurability". Under the binary test, blocking power therefore comes out an <strong>invariant</strong>.
  But that is wrong: a different repository, whose pull requests never touch this one's land path, may set
  its own blocking set freely and be entirely coherent. The binary test cannot say so, because it never asks
  <strong>over what scope</strong> the two consumers differ.
</p>
<p>
  So the answer is not binary. <strong>Every lever is a setting at some scope.</strong> The question is
  <em>which scope it is pinned at</em>, and "platform invariant" is the special case where the pin sits above
  every scope any consumer controls.
</p>

<h3>Four questions, in order</h3>
<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Is it a lever at all?</strong> Can you name one consumer who could coherently set it to a value
    other than ours? If yes, it belongs in the catalogue.
    <p>
      <strong>Cost and local fit are not answers to this question.</strong> "We would not use it" and "it is
      too expensive here" are <em>setting values</em>, not membership tests. The only admissible exclusion is
      <strong>incoherence</strong> — a value no consumer could hold without the system contradicting itself.
      The two rejections must stay visibly apart, because collapsing them is how a cost argument returns
      wearing a merit costume.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Name the two scopes.</strong> The <em>decision scope</em> is the object whose fate the lever
    decides (this finding, this pull request, this repo's policy). The <em>setting scope</em> is where a
    consumer would turn the knob (per juror, per run, per surface, per repo).
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Push the pin up until the conflict clears.</strong> If varying the lever across the setting scope
    could give <strong>one object two conflicting fates</strong>, the lever is pinned one scope up. Repeat.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Is the pin above every consumer scope?</strong> It is a <strong>platform invariant</strong> if and
    only if varying it anywhere would make the system's own output vocabulary <strong>mean different things
    in two places that share it</strong>. Not "would be a worse policy" — <em>would make the word
    unreadable</em>.
  </li>
</ol>

<h3>Two riders the evidence forces</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Rider A — an axis is ordered by value, never by goodness.</strong> Recording a range must not imply
  that turning it up is better. The only controlled experiment on change decomposition (di Biase et al.,
  PeerJ CS 2019, n=28) found <strong>no</strong> effect on defects found (p=0.6), <strong>no</strong> effect on
  review time (p=0.66) and <strong>no</strong> effect on rationale understanding; its single significant
  result was false positives falling 6 to 1 (p=0.03, Cliff's δ=0.36). Ribeiro measured a plain union of three
  analyzers dropping precision from <strong>0.57 to 0.39</strong> against the best single member. So
  <em>more reviewers</em> is not itself a quality lever, and every row carries what it is evidenced to buy —
  or an honest blank.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Rider B — a null result travels with its lever</strong>, recorded in the same row, so a consumer
  setting the lever sees both sides.
</p>

<h3>The refutation — it mis-classifies a ruling made one day earlier</h3>
<p>
  Run the four questions on the diff-size <em>refuse</em> threshold. Q1 admits it (a refuse-at-900 threshold is
  coherent, and cost is inadmissible). Q2: decision scope is the pull request, setting scope is the repo. Q3: no
  pull request gets two conflicting fates, so the pin stays. Q4: no shared word becomes unreadable.
  <strong>Verdict: a repo-scope consumer setting.</strong>
</p>
<p>
  The actual ruling was the opposite, and not on cost: <em>"It is not even a config dimension… a
  supported-but-unset refuse threshold invites a future reader to switch it on for the wrong reason.
  <strong>There is no value of it that is correct, so the knob is not built.</strong>"</em> The ground was that
  the 400-line figure is a <strong>human attention</strong> ceiling, and attention is the one property an agent
  panel does not share with a human reviewer.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>So there is a third exclusion ground, and the test's first question forbids it:
  domain-misapplication of the underlying proxy.</strong> A lever may be excluded not because it is expensive
  (never admissible), and not because no consumer could hold any value coherently, but because
  <strong>the quantity it measures does not exist in this domain</strong>. That ground survives with cost set to
  zero, so it is not a cost argument in disguise — and it is this survey's one durable contribution to
  classification. The test itself is <strong>withdrawn rather than patched</strong>: a classifier that gets the
  neighbouring ratified case wrong has not earned reuse on a case nobody has decided.
</p>

