<h2>What the catalogue found that nobody had named</h2>
<ol>
  <li>
    <strong>Three homes, three different change-clearances, and the assignment does not track how
    load-bearing the lever is.</strong> The policy contract is human-gated; the escalation rubric is
    agent-clearable; the jury core sits in no policy tier at all. So a 400-line size threshold that was ruled
    unable to refuse anything is human-gated, while the eight numbers that <em>consume</em> it are not.
    <strong>The genuinely escaped set is exactly three constants</strong> — the impact bar that stops a land,
    the evidence floor, and mandatory-lens <em>membership</em>.
    <em>Corrected:</em> an earlier draft added "and every round cap", reasoning from the path roster alone.
    The care-to-jury conformance suite is itself spec-leashed and pins each band's lenses, jurors-per-lens and
    round cap, so the caps have an indirect but real human backstop. Membership escapes because the suite pins
    band lenses as a <em>subset of the five-lens panel set</em>, and that set is the <strong>union</strong> of
    the mandatory and advisory lists — so moving the security lens from one list to the other leaves the union
    byte-identical and the suite green, while removing that lens's power to block.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The mandatory-lens set is a free parameter, not a ruled constant.</strong> The panel reducer
    defaults it and validates only that it is non-empty, so any caller may seat a panel in which no lens holds
    the any-impact veto.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Seating already varies by surface; blocking power varies nowhere.</strong> The parked-PR loop's
    own mandatory literal is <em>identical</em> to the core's — what differs is the <em>seated</em> roster,
    four lenses against five. The seating-versus-blocking split is therefore observable in the tree, not just
    asserted in a ruling.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>There are two independent impact bars, not one</strong> — one governing which findings block, one
    governing which findings escape the evidence floor. They can be set in opposite directions and nothing
    relates them.
  </li>
  <li><strong>Three round-cap constants</strong>, plus an editor round floor that interacts with all three.</li>
  <li>
    <strong>Per-reviewer change size is the best-evidenced axis in the literature — and the use this survey
    first made of it was wrong.</strong> Baum et al. (EMSE 2019, n=50 professionals) measured review
    effectiveness at <strong>59% on small changes versus 35% on large</strong>, efficiency 15.65 against 9.47
    defects/hour, and a delocalized defect found in <strong>76%</strong> of small reviews against
    <strong>36%</strong> of large ones (McNemar p = 0.002 / 0.001 / 0.0117 / 0.0034, all surviving
    Bonferroni-Holm).
    <em>Retracted in full:</em> this originally claimed to reconcile the size ruling with the scoped-fan-out
    story, and that <em>"neither card says this"</em>. Both halves are false. That story's opening line already
    states the reconciliation, and it explicitly <strong>forbids</strong> the remedy the argument implied —
    <em>"truncating a juror's diff to its shard would reintroduce exactly the blindness the fan-out exists to
    remove"</em>. Every juror in that design is deliberately still a whole-diff reviewer. Worse, the size
    ruling's own anchor states that the 400-line figure is an <em>attention</em> ceiling and that attention is
    the one property an agent panel does not share with a human — so citing a human-attention study to size an
    agent panel commits the exact error that anchor was written to prevent.
    <strong>What survives:</strong> the numbers are real evidence about <em>human</em> reviewers, and whether
    they transfer to an agent panel is precisely the question already answered <em>no</em>.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Seat accountability is not modelled at all.</strong> Jurors are anonymous positions. Meta
    (arXiv 2312.17169) measured assigning a named individual rather than a pool at <strong>11.6% less
    time-in-review with no quality-guardrail regression</strong>; pooling causes diffusion of responsibility.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>What occupies the first position of a diff is an accident</strong> of alphabetical ordering — and
    the evidence narrows the lever rather than endorsing the obvious fix. Fregnan et al. (ESEC/FSE 2022,
    n=106, Distinguished Paper) found <strong>64% lower odds</strong> of catching a seeded defect when its
    file was shown <em>last</em>, and hot-spot-first ordering yielded <strong>+23% review comments</strong>
    (n=29). But Baum (EMSE 2019, n=50) tested general dependency-optimal reordering against deliberately bad
    orders and found <strong>no statistical confirmation</strong> (p = 0.2587 / 0.1084 / 0.0537) —
    participants <em>perceived</em> bad orders as chaos without performing better under good ones. The
    evidenced lever is therefore <em>which subset leads</em>, not how the whole diff is sorted.
  </li>
</ol>

<h2>Membership: what stays in the space, and the one thing that does not</h2>
<p>
  Four options had been struck from the design space on <strong>cost</strong> or <strong>local fit</strong>.
  Three are re-admitted as range values, because neither is a membership test:
</p>
<we-data-table>
<table class="data-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Struck as</th><th>Re-admitted as</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>"about $0.43 per run is too expensive" — live-repeated stability measurement</td><td>A range value on <em>how stability is measured</em>. This project happens to set it to "replayed".</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"needs symbol-level analysis, deliberately out of scope" — dependency-graph shard cutting</td><td>A range value on <em>shard cut basis</em>. Whether its merit argument survives belongs to the fan-out item; the cost half must not remove it from the space.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>"built the dial and shipped it off" — the evidence floor</td><td>Not a removal at all: this is the <strong>correct</strong> shape, and the worked example of it — lever present, setting permissive.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>the cheap/expensive framing was struck — a named minimum fixture set</td><td><strong>No axis survives underneath it.</strong> Checked and reported as a negative result rather than a manufactured row.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</we-data-table>
<p>
  <strong>The exclusion that still stands, kept visibly distinct:</strong> a value <em>no consumer could
  coherently hold</em> is not a lever. "An unrun adversary ratifies" is the example. That is incoherence, not
  expense — and keeping the two rejections apart is the whole point, because collapsing them is how a cost
  argument comes back disguised as merit.
</p>

<h2>Honest verdict on the framing — it is not load-bearing</h2>
<p>
  A skeptic pass, instructed to try exactly this attack, argued that "levers and settings" is a tidy
  re-description that changes no decision. <strong>Every load-bearing claim it made was then checked against
  the tree, and it was right.</strong> Three claimed leverage points, all failed:
</p>
<ol>
  <li>
    <em>"It changes what the blocking-scan build should do."</em> <strong>Manufactured.</strong> The
    instruction to home that list in the contract was already written by the sibling decision that
    <em>gates</em> that build. The delta predates this survey; claiming it is claiming credit for a change this
    work did not cause.
  </li>
  <li>
    <em>"It exposes the clearance asymmetry."</em> <strong>Half true, and the wrong half was claimed.</strong>
    The asymmetry is real and was found here — but finding it required <em>reading the files</em>, not
    classifying them, and the repo's ratified contract-split rule already supplies both the diagnosis and the
    fix.
  </li>
  <li>
    <em>"It reconciles the size ruling with scoped fan-out."</em> <strong>Retracted</strong> — see finding 6.
  </li>
</ol>
<p>
  <strong>Run against the four open cards it was meant to help, the score is zero of four.</strong> One had
  already performed the scope analysis in its own words and dated earlier; the second changes only through the
  first; the third's blocker is an unwritten executable criterion, which no classification supplies; and the
  fourth turns entirely on measured false-positive rates, which the test's first question rules inadmissible —
  so it would return nothing about the only axis that card turns on.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>What the work is worth, without inflation.</strong> Reading 222 constants end-to-end found two real
  defects in a ratified trust boundary, one of which is a hole in the <em>pattern</em> rather than in this
  instance, plus a third exclusion ground the repo was using without having written down. Those are the
  deliverables. The vocabulary contributed nothing to them, and about forty-five of the sixty-five rows are
  inventory with no consequence attached. <strong>A survey worth its cost, wrapped in a framing that was
  not</strong> — reported that way because shipping the framing on the strength of the survey it happens to sit
  next to is precisely the overclaim this programme exists to catch.
</p>

<h2>Coverage — what this survey read, and what it did not</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Predicate:</strong> a lever is a named identifier or contract field the review pipeline reads, whose
  value would change what gets looked at, by whom, what a finding must carry, how findings reduce, or what the
  verdict does. <strong>Candidate set:</strong> the jury core, the escalation rubric, the policy contract, the
  gate config, the review CLI, the pull-request review operation, the parked-PR workflow, the convergence CLI,
  the review anchors in the platform-decisions statute, and the open cards under the review-efficacy watch.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Not covered.</strong> Merge mechanics and drain scheduling; CI configuration; anything outside this
  repository; <strong>prompt wording</strong> — every juror mandate is a lever and this survey counts "the
  mandate" as one row rather than enumerating its sentences, which is the largest single gap; surfaces that
  consume review output rather than produce it; and any lever whose only home is a lane clone or a
  machine-local ledger. Sixty-five rows — rolled up from 222 individually-tunable constants and parameters —
  is a count produced by that predicate, not a claim of completeness. Where the roll-up hides a divergence,
  that is a defect of this write-up, not of the pipeline; the divergences are called out individually above
  rather than smoothed away.
</p>

